const codingProblems = [
  { language: 'PYTHON', difficulty: 'EASY', title: 'Count values above the mean', statement: 'Given a non-empty list of numeric values, return how many are strictly greater than the arithmetic mean. For [2, 4, 6], the answer is 1.', solution: 'Compute mean = sum(values)/len(values), then sum(value > mean for value in values). Time O(n), extra space O(1). Check empty input according to the function contract.' },
  { language: 'PYTHON', difficulty: 'EASY', title: 'First non-repeating character', statement: 'Return the first character that appears once in a string, or None if none exists. Example: "swiss" → "w".', solution: 'Use collections.Counter to count characters, then scan the original string in order and return the first whose count is 1. O(n) expected time and O(k) space.' },
  { language: 'PYTHON', difficulty: 'MEDIUM', title: 'Merge overlapping intervals', statement: 'Given intervals such as [[1,3],[2,6],[8,10]], merge overlaps to produce [[1,6],[8,10]]. Treat touching endpoints consistently and state the chosen convention.', solution: 'Sort by start. Append the first interval; for each next interval, extend the last end when it overlaps, otherwise append. O(n log n) due to sorting.' },
  { language: 'PYTHON', difficulty: 'MEDIUM', title: 'Validate balanced brackets', statement: 'Check whether (), [], and {} are correctly nested. Return false for a closing bracket without a matching opener.', solution: 'Push opening brackets on a stack. On a closer, compare with the top using a map; fail on empty/mismatch. Return whether the stack is empty. O(n) time, O(n) space.' },
  { language: 'SQL', difficulty: 'EASY', title: 'Average score by subject', statement: 'Given Result(student_id, subject_id, score), return subject_id and average score for subjects with at least five results.', solution: 'SELECT subject_id, AVG(score) AS average_score FROM Result GROUP BY subject_id HAVING COUNT(*) >= 5; HAVING filters grouped rows.' },
  { language: 'SQL', difficulty: 'MEDIUM', title: 'Students without submissions', statement: 'Given Student(id, name) and Submission(student_id), list students with no submissions.', solution: 'Use LEFT JOIN and WHERE Submission.student_id IS NULL, or NOT EXISTS correlated on student id. NOT EXISTS avoids duplicate output if submissions repeat.' },
  { language: 'JAVA', difficulty: 'EASY', title: 'Find the second-largest distinct value', statement: 'Return the second-largest distinct integer or an empty OptionalInt if fewer than two distinct values exist.', solution: 'Track largest and second distinct values in one pass, using explicit initialization/OptionalInt rather than sentinel assumptions. O(n) time, O(1) extra space.' },
  { language: 'JAVA', difficulty: 'MEDIUM', title: 'Group words by anagram signature', statement: 'Group words that contain the same letters, ignoring case. Preserve the input words in the returned groups.', solution: 'Normalize each word, sort its characters for a canonical key, and append the original word to a map entry. O(n·m log m) for n words of length m.' },
  { language: 'CPP', difficulty: 'EASY', title: 'Rotate a vector right by k', statement: 'Rotate [1,2,3,4,5] right by 2 positions to get [4,5,1,2,3]. Handle k larger than vector length and empty input.', solution: 'If empty return. Reduce k modulo size and use std::rotate at begin() + (n-k), end(). Complexity O(n). Include <algorithm>.' },
  { language: 'CPP', difficulty: 'MEDIUM', title: 'Top-k frequent values', statement: 'Return the k most frequent integers; define how ties are ordered.', solution: 'Count with unordered_map, then use a min-heap of size k ordered by frequency and documented tie-break. O(n log k) expected time.' },
  { language: 'DSA', difficulty: 'EASY', title: 'Binary search insertion point', statement: 'For a sorted ascending array and target, return its first index if present or the index where it should be inserted.', solution: 'Use lower_bound on the half-open range [lo, hi): compare middle value, shrink one side, and return lo. O(log n); correctness depends on sorted input.' },
  { language: 'DSA', difficulty: 'EASY', title: 'Queue using two stacks', statement: 'Implement enqueue and dequeue with two stacks; dequeue must return the oldest item.', solution: 'Push new items to input stack. When output is empty, move all input items to output; pop output. Amortized O(1) per operation.' },
  { language: 'DSA', difficulty: 'MEDIUM', title: 'Shortest path in an unweighted graph', statement: 'Given adjacency lists and source/target, return the shortest number of edges or -1 when unreachable.', solution: 'BFS using a queue; mark each node when enqueued and store distance. Each vertex and edge is processed once: O(V+E).' },
  { language: 'PYTHON', difficulty: 'MEDIUM', title: 'Moving average with a window', statement: 'Return the arithmetic average for each consecutive window of width w. Reject w <= 0 or w > n.', solution: 'Compute the first window sum, then update by adding the incoming and subtracting outgoing value. O(n) time, O(n-w+1) output.' },
  { language: 'SQL', difficulty: 'MEDIUM', title: 'Latest row per account', statement: 'Given Event(account_id, occurred_at, id, value), return one latest event per account. Define a deterministic tie-break.', solution: 'Use ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY account_id ORDER BY occurred_at DESC, id DESC) and keep row_number=1.' },
  { language: 'JAVA', difficulty: 'EASY', title: 'Palindrome after normalization', statement: 'Check whether a string is a palindrome ignoring case and non-alphanumeric characters.', solution: 'Move two indices inward, skip non-alphanumeric code points as specified, compare normalized characters. State whether Unicode code points or ASCII are supported.' },
  { language: 'CPP', difficulty: 'EASY', title: 'First duplicate in a stream', statement: 'Return the first value whose second occurrence appears while scanning left to right, or no value.', solution: 'Insert into unordered_set; on failed insertion return current value. Expected O(n) time and O(n) space.' },
  { language: 'DSA', difficulty: 'MEDIUM', title: 'Detect a cycle in a directed graph', statement: 'Determine whether a directed graph contains a cycle.', solution: 'Use DFS with white/gray/black states; an edge to gray indicates a back-edge and cycle. O(V+E) time and O(V) state.' },
];

